---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const names = [
  ['Ivone Mota Figueiredo', 'Her birth name in full — the Supreme Court of the Transvaal, 1948'],
  ['Ivone de Figueiredo', 'As the 1948 Johannesburg marriage register has it'],
  ['Ivone / Mota “Yvonne” Figueiredo', 'As the family tree carries her — “Mota” unexplained, “Yvonne” the anglicisation Johannesburg gave her'],
  ['Ivone de Witt', 'The name she married Kurt Luwinski under, in June 1948'],
  ['Ivone Luwinski', 'The name she married Morris Salkow under, in February 1953'],
  ['Ivone, formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo', 'The Supreme Court of the Transvaal, 1951'],
];
const marriages = [
  ['18 Nov 1939', 'Frederico Pinto da Rocha', 'Lourenço Marques, 1.ª Conservatória — dissolved by divorce, sentence of 6 Dec 1945', 'Son Henrique, born 11 March 1941, sixteen months after the wedding'],
  ['2 Feb 1948', 'Johannes Antonie Oelofse de Witt', 'b. Dec 1923 Johannesburg; d. Sasolburg', 'Germiston'],
  ['19 Jun 1948', 'Kurt Israel Luwinski', 'b. 1912 Berlin; d. 1968 Johannesburg', 'Johannesburg, cert. 858'],
  ['13 Feb 1953', 'Morris Isaac Salkow', 'b. 12 Oct 1921 Pretoria; d. 9 Nov 1991 Johannesburg, buried West Park', 'He sued her for divorce in the same year \u2014 WLD 2225/1953. The marriage lasted months.'],
];
---
<Base title="Ivone de Figueiredo"
  subtitle="Lourenço Marques, 4 March 1922 — Vila Nova de Gaia, March 2010"
  lede="She was born in a Portuguese colonial town on the Indian Ocean, had a son at seventeen, married four men in Johannesburg between 1948 and 1953 — two of them Jewish, one of them an Afrikaner, the first a Portuguese she left behind — and was buried at the other end of the empire, in Porto.">

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>She is filed under six spellings, and the indexes carry the worst of them <span class="tag doc">16 Sep 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>IVONE, YVONNE, IVONNE. FIGUEIREDO, DE FIGUEIREDO, MOTA FIGUEIREDO, DE FIGUERIDO,
      DE FIGUEREDO, FIGARETTO. DE WITT, LUWINSKI, LUWINSKY, LUWENSKI, SALKOW.</strong>
      Fourteen forms of one woman&rsquo;s name, each taken from a record that names her.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not a curiosity. <strong>The one file in which she is the plaintiff rather than the
      defendant &mdash; TAB WLD 2545/1950, in which she sued Kurt &mdash; is indexed as
      <em>IVONE LUWENSKI, born FIGARETTO</em></strong>, and was invisible for months to every search
      run on her actual name. Neither word is a spelling anyone would choose.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Every form, with the record it comes from and whether it is a document variant or an index
      corruption, is set out on <a href={base + '/names/'}>her names</a>.
    </p>
  </div>


  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Her name is Mota Figueiredo <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree calls her <em>Ivone / Mota “Yvonne” Figueiredo</em>, and everyone — this archive
      included, until the South African national archives index was read properly — took
      <strong>Mota</strong> for a stray nickname sitting next to the anglicised <em>Yvonne</em>.
    </p>
    <p>It is not a nickname. It is half her surname. The Transvaal Supreme Court, 1948:</p>
    <blockquote>Illiquid case divorce. <strong>Kurt Luwinsky</strong> versus <strong>Ivone (born Mota Figueiredo)</strong>.<br />
      <span class="small">— National Archives of South Africa, Transvaal depot, Witwatersrand Local Division, <strong>WLD 1950/1948</strong></span></blockquote>
    <p>
      She was born <strong>Ivone Mota Figueiredo</strong>, in all likelihood <em>Ivone da Mota Figueiredo</em>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Which gives us her parents' surnames</h2>
  <p>
    Portuguese naming order puts the <strong>mother's</strong> surname before the
    <strong>father's</strong>. Her son's name already demonstrated the rule working in this family —
    <em>Henrique <strong>Figueiredo</strong> <strong>Pinto da Rocha</strong></em>, his mother's name then
    his father's. Applied to her own:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Surname</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Her father</strong></td><td>a <strong>FIGUEIREDO</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Her mother</strong></td><td>a <strong>MOTA</strong> — most likely <em>da Mota</em></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    It is the first thing this archive has ever known about Ivone's parents, and it came out of an
    index entry eleven words long. <span class="tag inf">Inferred from a documented name</span>
  </p>

  <h2>The names</h2>
  <p>
    She appears in the record under five, which is itself the story. The constant is
    <strong>Figueiredo</strong>: the name she was born with, and the one the court reached for in 1951
    when it had to say who she actually was.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>As written</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{names.map(([n, w]) => (<tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td>{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Her son's name gives up her father's <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      Her first son was <strong>Henrique Figueiredo Pinto da Rocha</strong>. Portuguese naming puts the
      mother's surname before the father's. So <em>Figueiredo</em> is hers, and <em>Pinto da Rocha</em>
      is the father's — the man the tree can only call “Unknown da Rocha”.
    </p>
    <p class="small">Which makes <strong>Pinto da Rocha</strong>, not <em>da Rocha</em>, the name to search on.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Lourenço Marques, 1922</h2>
  <p>
    Lourenço Marques — Maputo since 1976 — was the capital of Portuguese East Africa, a deep-water port
    built on the traffic of the Witwatersrand goldfields: the Rand's nearest sea, joined to Johannesburg
    by rail since 1895. A Portuguese family in Lourenço Marques in 1922 was living four hundred miles
    from Johannesburg on a line that ran straight there, which is the plainest available answer to how
    she came to be married in Germiston twenty-four years later.
  </p>

  <h2>Three court files, all now identified</h2>
  <p>
    Kurt sued her three times. The national archives index has all three, in the Transvaal depot,
    and they can be ordered.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Year</th><th>As the index has it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>WLD 1950/1948</strong></td><td>1948</td><td>Divorce. Kurt <em>Luwinsky</em> v. Ivone <strong>(born Mota Figueiredo)</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>WLD 497/1951</strong></td><td>1951</td><td>Restitution of conjugal rights. Kurt Luwinski v. Ivone (formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>WLD 2368/1951</strong></td><td>1951</td><td>Divorce. Kurt Luwinski v. Ivone (formerly de Witt) (born Figueiredo)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    Watch what changes. In <strong>1948 she is not “formerly de Witt”</strong> — only <em>born Mota
    Figueiredo</em>. By 1951 she is. Either the first clerk left it out, or the de Witt marriage was
    not being recognised in the earlier action. A divorce summons of this period normally recites both
    parties' full names, ages and domiciles and the date and place of the marriage, so these three
    files are worth ordering on their own account.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The 1951 divorce was granted <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The index lists the actions; it does not say how they ended. That is now settled from outside the
      court. When Kurt married again on <strong>29 February 1956</strong>, the registrar entered his
      personal status as <strong>Divorcee</strong> and noted in the Remarks that an
      <strong>order</strong> had been <strong>filed</strong> — the decree he had to produce before a
      marriage officer would proceed. A registrar in Johannesburg saw the document that ended this
      marriage and wrote that he had seen it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Which also dates the end of Ivone's second marriage to <strong>somewhere between 1951 and
      February 1956</strong>, and she had already remarried inside that window — Morris Isaac Salkow,
      13 February 1953. See <a href={base + "/kurt/"}>Kurt</a> for the register entry in full.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Four marriages</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Husband</th><th>Him</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{marriages.map(([d, h, w, p]) => (<tr><td>{d}</td><td><strong>{h}</strong></td><td>{w}</td><td>{p}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The Germiston register, read at last <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      It was attached to the family tree as a photograph and never opened. Register <strong>no. 34
      of 1948</strong>, Germiston, <strong>2 February 1948</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Johannes Antonie Oelofse de Witt</th><th>Yvonne Figueiredo</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>25</td><td><strong>22</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Born</td><td>Transvaal</td><td><strong>Lourenço Marques</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Personal status</strong></td><td>Bachelor</td><td><strong>SPINSTER</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Miner, underground</strong></td><td><strong>Dressmaker — firm</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td>77 Browning Street, Fairview</td><td><strong>42 Joel Road, Berea</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">
      Married by <strong>special licence no. 156884</strong>, dated the same day, without antenuptial
      contract. Witnesses <strong>B. Green</strong> and <strong>E. E. Naude</strong>; marriage officer
      <strong>A. J. Rossouw</strong>, Magistrate's Office, Germiston. Both parties signed:
      <em>J.A.O. de Witt</em> and <em>Y. Figueiredo</em> — so the Y-spelling is not only what officials
      wrote about her, it is what she wrote herself.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Read again at full resolution.</strong> The first reading of this frame was made from the
      1,195-pixel copy the website serves; the original is <strong>4,931 × 3,929</strong>. Five cells
      came out differently and have been corrected here: the register number (34, not 54), the licence
      number, both witnesses, and the husband's address. <span class="tag disp">Self-corrected</span>
      Nothing in the argument below changes — <em>spinster</em>, <em>Lourenço Marques</em> and the age
      of 22 all read the same at either size.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The da Rocha marriage, and what she declared at Germiston <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree records <em>“Marriage to: Unknown Da Rocha, before 1941”</em>, and
      <strong>her own birth registration names him and dates it.</strong> Ivone married
      <strong>FREDERICO PINTO DA ROCHA on 18 November 1939</strong> at the 1.ª Conservatória do Registo
      Civil de Lourenço Marques. The marriage was dissolved by divorce, by a sentence of
      <strong>6 December 1945</strong>. Both facts are marginal notes on the assento, added in 1987.
    </p>
    <p>
      So at Germiston in February 1948 she was <strong>not a spinster but a divorcée of two
      years</strong> — and the register there understates more than her status. It gives her age as
      <strong>22</strong>; born on 4 March 1922 she was <strong>25</strong>, a fortnight short of 26.
      A woman remarrying in another country, with a six-year-old son left behind in Mozambique, gave
      an office the version of herself she wanted on the page. The Portuguese state was not consulted
      and went on recording her as <em>divorciado</em> until she died.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Henrique was born on 11 March 1941, sixteen months after that wedding and four years
      before the divorce.</strong> He carries Pinto da Rocha because his parents were married to each
      other when he was born — which is a plainer reason than a father acknowledging him by surname.
      His father has a forename now, and it is not an inference: <strong>Frederico</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      A husband at the date of birth is a legal presumption and not a certificate, and nothing here
      excludes another man. What it changes is the search. His birth registration at Lourenço
      Marques is no longer the <strong>only</strong> document likely to name his father, because
      <strong>the marriage entry of 18 November 1939 exists too</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the gap is four months and seventeen days <span class="tag disp">Unexplained</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>2 February 1948: spinster, marries de Witt.</strong><br />
      <strong>19 June 1948: divorced, marries Kurt Luwinski.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Both are on official registers, both signed by her. A divorce granted inside four and a half
      months was not ordinary in South Africa in 1948, and this archive does not understand how it
      was done — annulment, a void marriage, or a court moving faster than courts moved.
      <strong>The Germiston divorce file is the next document.</strong> And the pattern then repeats
      at once: Kurt filed against her within weeks of their own wedding.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And what became of the first husband <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      He has always ended mid-sentence on this page: a miner of 77 Browning Street, Fairview, who
      married her in February and is never heard of again. Two records found on 20 September 2026
      finish him.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>He remarried on 25 February 1950</strong> &mdash; <em>Johannes Antonie Oelofse Dewitt
      and <strong>Elsie Marie Geyer</strong></em>, in the national civil marriage index, he aged 27
      and she born about 1930. And <strong>he died on 2 April 1963 at Sasolburg</strong> in the
      Orange Free State, aged 40, in the civil death register, which gives his birthplace as
      <strong>Pretoria</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      That last date matters beyond him. The family tree holds this man <em>twice</em> &mdash; once
      as himself with no date of death, and once as his own son with the date &mdash; and neither
      record carries a citation. A government death register now says what the uncited duplicate
      said, which is what turns a tidy inference into a fact.
      <a href={base + '/evidence/'}>The correction, and what it still does not settle &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      One loose thread it pulls. Three officials wrote down his age &mdash; 25 in February 1948, 27
      in February 1950, 40 in April 1963 &mdash; and the three ranges overlap at April 1922 to
      February 1923. The tree&rsquo;s &ldquo;December 1923, Johannesburg&rdquo; is outside all three,
      and the death register says Pretoria. Declared ages are not proof of anything on their own.
      Three of them agreeing is worth writing down.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>They were in the same trade <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      She was a <strong>dress maker</strong>. Kurt, on his 1933 emigration record, was a
      <strong>dress designer</strong> — his mother's trade — and by 1948 a shop keeper. Two people in
      the Johannesburg rag trade, four months apart on the same page of her life.
      <strong>It is the first plausible account of how they met</strong>, and it is pleasingly
      ordinary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the two registers showed about the dates <span class="tag doc">Resolved</span></h3>
    <p>
      The archive previously called these dates a contradiction and guessed the February one was
      wrong. <strong>It was not.</strong> Both marriages are real, both registers have been read, and
      the four-and-a-half-month gap is exactly what it looks like. What was wrong was the assumption
      that no divorce could fit inside it.
    </p>
    <div class="panel" style="margin-top:0">
      <h3>And the register itself now answers it <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
      <p>
        The 1948 entry has been read — it was attached to the family tree as a photograph, unopened.
        Column 5, <em>Personal Status</em>, gives Kurt as <strong>Bachelor</strong> and Ivone as
        <strong>DIVORCED</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        <strong>She was not still married to de Witt in June 1948.</strong> That marriage had ended.
        So the February 1948 date is wrong, as suspected — the de Witt marriage was earlier, and she
        came to the second one a divorcée, under her first married name.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The Luwinski date is solid: it is on the certificate, number 858, with her age given as 24. The
      February date carries the <em>same</em> citation as the June one, which means it was never
      independently sourced. The likeliest reading is that the de Witt marriage was earlier and the
      February 1948 date belongs to something else — its dissolution, most obviously. But she was
      already <em>Mrs de Witt</em> when she married Kurt, so the de Witt marriage is real and came first.
      <strong>The Germiston register settles it.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The son she left, and the son she kept</h2>
  <p>
    Henrique was born on 11 March 1941, when she had just turned nineteen. He did not reach Johannesburg until
    <strong>November 1962</strong> — twenty-one years later, and a grown man. In between, his mother had
    married three more times in another country and had a second son there. Henrique married
    Dawn Elizabeth Hendey on 18 May 1968, and died on 27 October 2022, outliving his wife by thirteen
    years.
  </p>

  <h2>Her cousins are alive, and there are dozens of them</h2>
  <p>
    Two of the Portuguese DNA matches were opened in full. Both resolve to common
    ancestors at <strong>great-grandparent depth</strong> — which is exactly where Ivone's own parents
    and grandparents sit. Match holders are living and are not named here; their families' surnames are,
    because those are the search terms.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Match</th><th>Shared DNA</th><th>Best reading</th><th>Shared matches with Derrick</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A match in Portugal, b. 1974</td><td><strong>108.7 cM</strong>, 5 segments</td><td><strong>2nd cousin's son, 52.1%</strong> — common ancestors are <strong>great-grandparents</strong></td><td><strong>36</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A match in Portugal, b. 1949</td><td><strong>133.2 cM</strong>, 4 segments</td><td>3rd cousin 41%; 2nd cousin 17%</td><td><strong>37</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the closer one implies <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      If the second-cousin's-son reading holds, then Derrick and that match's mother — born 1952, died
      2018 — are <strong>second cousins</strong>, sharing one set of great-grandparents. Derrick's
      Portuguese great-grandparents are <strong>Ivone's grandparents</strong>. So Ivone's grandparents
      should sit among the forebears of that family, whose recorded surnames are
      <strong>Jorge</strong>, <strong>Silvestre</strong> and <strong>Conde</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second match's line adds <strong>Gaspar</strong> and <strong>Matos</strong>, through a father
      born 1902 and dead in 1962.
    </p>
    <p class="small">Five surnames to take to the Portuguese registers, where before there were none.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    And the clusters do not touch. Every one of the seventy-odd shared matches across both lists is
    Portuguese — Ferrão, Dias, Fernandes, Fogaça da Mata, Pereira, Da Costa Oliveira, Amorim Meirelles,
    Queirós, Dos Santos Pais, Machado, Vargas, Carneiro, Matos, Souza Pereira, Pires. Not one British
    name appears among them. Derrick's matches split cleanly into a maternal Portuguese side and a
    paternal British one, which is <a href={base + '/dna/'}>the paternity finding arriving from the
    other direction</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A small human detail, and a warning for searchers</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The last marriage lasted months <span class="tag doc">16 September</span></h3>
    <p>
      This archive has carried Morris Isaac Salkow as the man in the house through Derrick&rsquo;s
      childhood. A sweep of the National Archives index found the file that ends that reading:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="small">
      <strong>TAB &middot; WLD 2225/1953</strong> &mdash; Illiquid case. <strong>Divorce.</strong>
      <strong>MORRIS ISAAC SALKOW versus IVONNE (born de Figueirido).</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>They married on 13 February 1953 and he sued her for divorce in the same year.</strong>
      The archive already knew the marriage ended &mdash; he married again in 1960 &mdash; but not
      that it ended almost at once. Johannesburg case numbers ran to five figures by the 1970s;
      2225 of 1953 is not a December filing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It explains the silence rather than deepening it.</strong> Nobody has been able to say
      where Ivone was between 1953 and her death at Porto, and that has been treated here as a
      failure of searching. It is more likely a fact about her life: the marriage that was supposed
      to settle her collapsed within the year, and after it she has no South African anchor at all.
      <span class="tag open">Next</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    In June 1948 she gave her age as <strong>24</strong>; in February 1948 she had given it as
    <strong>22</strong>. Four months apart, the two declarations differ by two years and neither is
    right: born on 4 March 1922 she was 25 in February and 26 in June. Her stated ages are not
    evidence of when she was born, only of what she was prepared to say.
    In February 1953 she gave it as <strong>25</strong>. She was 28. <strong>She took three years
    off.</strong> Any search keyed to a 1927 or 1928 birth year is chasing her, not a different woman.
  </p>
  <p>
    Her names accumulate rather than replace: by 1953 the register carries her as
    <em>Ivone de Figueredo de Witt Luwinski</em>, all three at once — which at least tells us the clerk
    had the earlier marriages in front of him.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And one name, from a passport register <span class="tag inf">Candidate only</span></h3>
    <p>
      Her father must be a Figueiredo. Searched across every Portuguese archive indexed on Digitarq,
      exactly one Figueiredo took a passport for <strong>Lourenço Marques</strong> before she was
      born: <strong>Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo</strong>, 21, of Eixo in Aveiro, on
      <strong>22 October 1904</strong>. It may well be nothing — Figueiredo is among the commonest
      names in Portugal and the index is a fraction of the whole.
      <a href={base + '/mozambique/'}>The case for and against him →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Porto</h2>
  <p>
    She died in the district of <strong>Porto</strong> — not Lourenço Marques, not Johannesburg, but
    the metropole, which she may never have lived in before. <strong>She died at home, at 04:30, at
    Rua Nova das Marinhas 82, Gulpilhares, Vila Nova de Gaia, in March 2010</strong>, of
    cardio-respiratory failure, with dementia recorded as a contributing condition of more than a
    year&rsquo;s standing. She was 88. The certificate gives her occupation as <em>doméstica</em>,
    and her marital status — sixty-five years after she left Mozambique — as <em>divorciado</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And it names her parents, which was the whole object of the exercise: JOAQUIM JÚLIO DE
    FIGUEIREDO and LEÓNIA DOS SANTOS MOTA.</strong> Her birth registration, supplied with it, goes a
    generation further and names all four grandparents — António Júlio de Figueiredo and Ana Pereira
    de Figueiredo, Augusto Mata and Júlia Durães Mata — and gives her parents&rsquo; home parishes in
    mainland Portugal: <strong>Vimieiro</strong> and <strong>Negozela</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    The day of the month is the one thing the certificate will not give up. The boxed digits read as
    the 16th or the 17th and this archive will not choose between them by eye; the certifying
    physician dated his signature 16 March 2010 and the papers were faxed that afternoon, which
    bounds it. The <em>assento de óbito</em> at Vila Nova de Gaia would settle the day.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Her line is the one part of this family the DNA confirms outright <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      Three of Derrick's ten closest matches are in Portugal, sharing 133, 111 and 109 centimorgans, all
      three flagged by the testing service as having Portugal in common with him. Against
      <strong>60.6% Portuguese</strong> in the ethnicity estimate. Whatever else is in doubt
      — and a great deal is — <a href={base + '/dna/'}>Ivone is not</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Why she is not simply looked up <span class="tag doc">Tested</span></h3>
    <p>
      A search of MyHeritage's birth, marriage and death collections for <em>Ivone Figueiredo</em>
      returns nine results: eight Brazilian, one Floridian. <strong>Portuguese and Portuguese-colonial
      civil registration is not in these indexes at all.</strong> Her records exist — in Porto, in
      Maputo, in Lisbon — and none of them can be reached from a desk. This is not a search done
      badly; it is a search that cannot succeed, and knowing which is worth something.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A coincidence in the same index, logged and not believed</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      Two Johannesburg files: <strong>WLD 1467/1971</strong>, a divorce —
      <em>Margaret Figueiredo (born <strong>Harrison</strong>) v. Estácio Figueiredo</em> — and
      <strong>WLD 506/1970</strong>, an ex parte application by the same woman.
